   "claims": [
    "Curving grades depends on the method used: For the linear rescaling, you add a fixed number of points for all students so that the best score is always 100%.",
    "Convert the resulting z-scores to new grades using the new mean and standard deviation: new grade = μ + z-score × σ The list of new grades is the following: 91.1, 91.1, 87.5, 76.8, 76.8, 73.2, 69.6, 69.6, 62.5, 51.7.",
    "It is a typical procedure if you want the highest score to be 100% or to ensure that most grades fall in the middle range (e.g., from B to D) even for a difficult exam.",
    "Typically, we want the best grade to equal 100.",
    "Here is the explanation for grades 80, 70, 90, 55, 60, and 80: Find the best score in the list — it’s 90; Evaluate the difference between 100 and the best score: 100 − 90 = 10.",
    "The final grades are: 90, 80, 100, 65, 70, 90."
   ],
